Ironsmith browser MSE renderer

The files under lib/mse are copied from Chiplis/ironsmith at revision
04f9864e93ed1550c563d0198564ad686e61d202, web/ui/src/lib/mse.
Source: https://github.com/Chiplis/ironsmith/tree/04f9864e93ed1550c563d0198564ad686e61d202/web/ui/src/lib/mse
Copyright remains with the Ironsmith contributors.
The upstream web/ui/package.json declares AGPL-3.0-or-later.
The complete AGPL version 3 text is included in LICENSE-AGPL-3.0-or-later.
upstream.json records each original source path and SHA-256 digest.

Manabrew additions are the public entry, declarations, special-layout
selection and field mapping, corpus metadata, package metadata, and
corpus-index generation script. These additions use AGPL-3.0-or-later.
The runtime retains upstream source except the local modifications recorded
in upstream.json. They cover failed-font cache eviction, fixed-paragraph
text fitting, artwork values in generated image expressions, linked Battle
and meld faces, unrelated reversible faces, and expansion-symbol transport
and absence handling. Text-free rules backings retain native layout and frame
painting while omitting only rules, flavor, chapter and level text glyphs.
Both modes expose the same rules fields with their loaded CSS fonts and ink.
Render caches distinguish the two modes. General modern planeswalkers use the
pack's mainframe template so ability text and stripe geometry remain aligned.
Land frames also use printing mana-production metadata; three-or-more-color
lands select solid gold rather than a multicolor gradient. Universes Beyond
promo metadata selects the native frame, and triangular security stamps map
to its native stamp.
No native MSE executable or Ironsmith host JSX is included.

Plane chaos fields include the pinned Planechase template's native
<margin:900:0:0> tag before initialization. Native combined text is evaluated
before its level fields; supplying the encoded margin preserves its symbol
clearance without changing the upstream ordered initialization semantics.

Printing selection rejects unsupported or ambiguous treatments instead of
substituting a generic frame. This includes modern Secret Lair records whose
metadata cannot distinguish conventional layouts from artwork-integrated text.
Identified retro and extended-art families remain eligible. Unknown layouts,
unavailable requested templates and incomplete multipart records use the
application's original-scan fallback; native templates do not guarantee exact
reproduction of every printing.

Corpus assets are separate from the renderer's software license.
The default source is MagicSetEditorPacks/Full-Magic-Pack at revision
16f1d13bb7f5924613fc1ddc25da582beccfefb6:
https://github.com/MagicSetEditorPacks/Full-Magic-Pack/tree/16f1d13bb7f5924613fc1ddc25da582beccfefb6
corpus-index.json contains file names and font-family metadata, not font,
frame, symbol, or template files. Browsers fetch required files directly
from the pinned raw.githubusercontent.com source at runtime. Public
availability does not establish redistribution rights. This package does
not grant rights to those assets or to Magic: The Gathering artwork.

VITE_MSE_CORPUS_URL may point to an independently authorized hosted corpus.
It must end with a slash and contain data/, Magic - Fonts/,
upstream-tree.json, and optionally runtime-index.json plus packages/.
The tree must be complete and match the pinned corpus revision. The optional
runtime index uses Ironsmith's deployment format. Cross-origin hosts must
allow CORS requests. Manabrew retains its require-corp isolation policy;
resource requests use CORS rather than bypassing that policy.

Default rendering needs network access. Cached original scans remain
available according to Manabrew's existing image cache policy, but this
runtime does not promise offline generated cards. To serve assets locally
or bundle them for offline use, obtain the relevant asset permissions first.

Regenerate metadata with yarn mse:corpus-index. This retrieves a blobless
Git tree and reads font files solely to extract family names; it does not
install or redistribute the corpus. Normal development, production, and
Tauri builds use the committed metadata and require no asset-install step.
